6.1 Vector Components
Henceforth, the Einstein summation convention will be employed.
A vector can be written as a linear combination of a basis, that is a list of linearly independent vectors the span the space. We are typically interested in vectors that belong to \(\b{E}^{n}\).
We typically denote orthonormal basis vectors with a tophat, for example \(\{\hat{\bs{E}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}\). Further, we will typically make use of orthonormal basis vectors unless specified otherwise.
Let \(\{\hat{\bs{E}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}\) form an orthonormal basis for \(\b{E}^{3}\). That is any vector \(\bs{u} \in \b{E}^{3}\) can be written as a linear combination of these basis vectors.
\[
\begin{align}
\bs{x}&=x_1 \hat{\bs{E}}_1+ x_2 \hat{\bs{E}}_2+ x_3 \hat{\bs{E}}_3\\
\bs{x}&=x_i \hat{\bs{E}}_i
\end{align}
\]
\[
\begin{align}
[\bs{x}]_{\{\hat{\bs{E}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}}&=
\left[
\begin{array}{c}
x_1\\
x_2\\
x_3
\end{array}
\right]
\end{align}
\]
Let \(\{\hat{\bs{F}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}\) form another orthonormal basis for \(\b{E}^{3}\). Then,
\[
\begin{align}
\bs{x}&=\tilde{x}_1 \hat{\bs{F}}_1+ \tilde{x}_2 \hat{\bs{F}}_2+ \tilde{x}_3 \hat{\bs{F}}_3\\
\bs{x}&=\tilde{x}_i \hat{\bs{F}}_i
\end{align}
\]
\[
\begin{align}
[\bs{x}]_{\{\hat{\bs{F}}_{i}\}_{i=1,2,3}}&=
\left[
\begin{array}{c}
\tilde{x}_1\\
\tilde{x}_2\\
\tilde{x}_3
\end{array}
\right]
\end{align}
\]
Notice that the components differ when the chosen basis differs. Additionally, for clarity, the matrix representation of the components may include a subscript denoting the basis. The following are equivalent representations of the vector:
\[
\begin{align}
\bs{x} = x_{i} \hat{\bs{E}}_{i} = \tilde{x}_{i} \hat{\bs{F}}_{i}
\end{align}
\]
The question is, are the components \(x_{i}\) and \(\tilde{x}_i\) the same? In general no. However, a relation between the two represenation can be determined using a change of basis, which will be discussed in a later section.

7.1 Trace
The trace is a map that maps a tensor to a real number and obeys the following rules: For any tensor \(\bs T\) and \(\bs S\) and any vectors \(\bs a, \bs b\), \[
    \begin{align}
        \rm{tr}\pr{\bs T + \bs S} &= \rm{tr} \bs T + \rm{tr} \bs S \\
        \rm{tr}\pr{\alpha \bs T} &= \alpha ~\rm{tr} \bs T \\
        \rm{tr}\pr{\bs{a} \otimes \bs{b}} &= \bs a \cdot \bs b
    \end{align}
\]
The first two properties of the trace show that the trace is a linear map.
In terms of tensor components,
\[
\begin{align}
\text{tr} \, \bs{T} = \text{tr}({T_{ij}}\bs{e}_i\bs{e}_j) = {T_{ij}}\text{tr}(\bs{e}_i\bs{e}_j) = {T_{ij}}\bs{e}_i\bs{e}_j = {T_{ij}}{\delta_{ij}} = {T_{ii}}.
\end{align}
\]
That is,
\[
\begin{align}
\text{tr} \, \bs{T} = {T_{11}} + {T_{22}} + {T_{33}} = \text{sum of diagonal elements}.
\end{align}
\]
It is, therefore, obvious that
\[
\begin{align}
\text{tr} \, \bs{T}^T = \text{tr} \, \bs{T}
\end{align}
\]

7.2 Determinant
The determinant is also a map that maps a tensor to a real number. Here, we give a definition of the determinant that is relevant and useful in the study of continuum mechanics. Let \(\bs T\) be a tensor that maps the three vectors \(\bs a, \bs b, \bs c\). The scalar triple product of the vectors \(\bs a, \bs b, \bs c\) is given by \[
\begin{align}
\abs{\bs a \cdot (\bs b \times \bs c)}
\end{align}
\]
which represents the volume of a parallelopiped constructed by the three vectors. Then the determinant of \(\bs{T}\), denoted \(\text{det} \, \bs T\) is given by \[
\begin{align}
\text{det} \, \bs T &= \frac{\abs{\bs T \bs a \cdot (\bs T \bs b \times \bs T \bs c)}}{\abs{\bs a \cdot (\bs b \times \bs c)}} .
\end{align}
\]
In component form, the determinant of \(\bs{T}\) is the determinant of the matrix of \(\bs{T}\). \[
\begin{align}
\text{det} \, \bs T &= \text{det} \, [\bs T]
\end{align}
\]
